			<tr>
				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody">Morani, &lt;Waylith’s voice fills the silence left by the end of a story&gt; what of you? &lt;Everyone’s eyes turn to the dark haired Wood Elf. Wandering into camp at evening’s crest, her weapons tied in a sign of peace, she asked to share their fire. As an elf, her dark eyes, high cheeks, and pointed ears blend into a characteristic beauty. Since she entered the camp, her face has worn a saddened reflection that looks both old companion and unwanted stranger.&gt;
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
        Ground Born, that’s what they call me. As if being born in the trees some makes them intrinsically closer to The Mother. My people can be such a haughty lot. And to think; it’s the Highs, not us, that have the reputation for that. GROUND Born….  Don’t get me wrong, I’m not outcast, I‘m just not as accepted as most. I guess I wouldn’t be so upset if I had not seen what I did last week. I was sitting near the Priest’s Lift when an Iksar sauntered past the guards, got on the lift and entered the City. The guards not only didn’t bat an eye about him being an Iksar, but they smiled WARMLY at him. 
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
Understand, I have no hatred for anyone (well, except Orcs and a particular Corrupted One that lives in Lower Faydark.) As a matter of fact, if it weren’t for an Iksar I literally ran into after I wandered too far into the home of the Crushbone Orcs (and attracted too much attention), I wouldn’t be here to complain. I never did get his name, but I owe him a life debt. 
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
Once again it seems I’ve wandered, this time it’s away from my point. What bothers me is that I’ve worked my WHOLE LIFE to gain the affection of my OWN people and an Iksar I’ve never seen before is totally accepted. I’m sure that Iksar worked hard for that, but why was I made to work as hard as that Iksar; because I’m Ground Born. 
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
My people are fiercely protective of each other and even our sisters to the south, the High Elves, have their places in our rumors and suspicions. Both of my parents where born Wood Elves in the Treed City, but because they had the urge to wander Tunari and didn’t make it back in time for my birth; I’m not QUITE part of them.
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
        Maybe I should start at the beginning. As my mentor Ran Sunfire used to tell me; “The beginning is the most wonderful place to be. Its the only place where the whole of Tunare is spread out before you.” As you might have guessed, I’m a Wood Elf but I wasn’t born in Kelethin or even Greater Faydark (what a wonderfully APT name for an often confusing and dangerous yet beautiful land of perpetual twilight). I wasn’t even born in Fedwer. I was born in the place called Halas. I’ve never been there since4, but I have a feeling it’s just a matter of time. 
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
My parents, so I’m told, were wandering the north and got caught by an early winter storm. They weren’t even married yet, they’d only met each other on the boat to Antonica. Conversation was started to pass the time during the voyage and turned to even longer conversations over land. Well, being stuck in a strange land with little more than each other for company, The Mother had her way and I was conceived. 
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
Fortunately, the unusually early start to winter was met with an unusually early end to it. Thus my parents were able to find their way to the Barbarian City where they married two weeks before I was born. Reluctant to travel back with a little one, they stayed there until I reached five years of age. 
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
I have very few memories of that time or the people but I remember their language and the gentleness with which they treated me. Looking back, I can now guess that they were probably afraid that they were going to crush such a small thing as an Elven girl. 
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
&lt;staring off into nowhere for a moment, Morani seems caught up in though as tears pool in her eyes and a smile touches her lips&gt; 
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
        We left right after the fist day of summer. Many Folk told us to stay, we were welcome, we’d made our home but I think my parents longed for the trees of their home. Eight years IS a long time to be away, even for an Elf. So, gathering what we needed, we headed south. For that summer and the better part of the fall, we traveled. So much of the journey is a blur to me. How I wish I could remember the details and walk it the other way. 
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
When we landed in Butcher Block, a change came over my parents. I was too young to understand it, but I could feel it. Looking back, I can only guess that what I felt was a mix of elation to be so near home again and dread at what they must have know was in store for me. You see, in the eyes of the Wood Elves, the People of the North are to uncivilized for any marriage they perform to be recognized. So, in their eyes, I’d be a bastard too.         
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
On the way though Butcher Block, they came up with a plan. My father had a friend in the High Elf City who was just becoming and accolade when he'd left. They would journey to Felwithe and see if my father’s friend would perform a marriage there and thus legitimize it before they went home. The only problem with the plan was, me. They were afraid that too much attention would be drawn by a couple with a child coming to seek marriage. That’s when Mother remembered a cousin of hers. He’d come up with this crazy idea of setting up a trading outpost in deeps of Lower Faydark. They could leave me with her cousin for a few days and come back for me. 
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
I will always remember the first time I saw the trees of Greater Faydark. They were to the trees I’d seen until then like the People of the North where to me as I crawled. Strong and majestic, seeming to reach their arms out in protection. We camped for four days on the southwest edge of Greater Faydark while my parents talked, sometimes heatedly, about the plan. Finally, they decided the danger was worth the risk and we traveled on.
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
Entering Lower Faydark with nothing more than an idea of where my mother’s cousin MIGHT still be, we quickly and quietly traveled south on the road. Two days journey had brought a close encounter with brownies and a harmless but frightening encounter with a group of monarch drakes. As night fell, my father made out the glint of a distant fire. Marking the position and guessing its distance, he and mother debated on pressing on. Deciding that the less time I was left with only their protection the better, we pressed on. Unfortunately, the fire belonged to a camp of Orcs. Fortunately, my father was a wise man and used the cover of night to sneak close enough to tell rather than just walking in and finding out the hard way. 
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
We circled the camp at a good distance and just as we were stopping to figure out what’s next, a jet-black mare came round the foot of a hill. A wave of evil could be felt washing over us. My father screamed; “RUN!!!!” as my mother scooped me up and carried me off. From over her shoulder, I saw my father drawing his sword as he was surrounded in red flame. The trees soon blocked my view and that was the last time I saw him. 
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
By complete accident, my mother stumbled into the outpost of her cousin. Quickly, they gathered a few of their strongest and ran out. Leaving me behind. 
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
Morning came and went. So did evening and the next night likewise. The following morning, my mother and the men that went with her dragged themselves in exhausted, battered, and saddened. They found sign of where my father fought Equestrielle The Corrupted (who’s name I was to find out latter), and where he died. Even though they searched all day, they never found his body.
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
We spent a month with them before they escorted us to the border of Greater Fay. From there, my mother and I traveled to Felwithe in near silence. She did manage to track down my father’s friend and after a long discussion I could almost here through a thick stone door, she talked him into performing the marriage ceremony. And then a funeral. 
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
As soon as the funeral was over, we left Felwithe for Kelethin. This was a mistake. My mother was overcome by grief and we lost our way. After she’d gotten herself together, we spent the rest of the day looking for landmarks she’d not seen in over 9 years. Night fell and we made camp. 
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
With the morning’s light, we started our search again. Within a few hours, things started to look familiar to her but she realized we’d wandered too far north. We were in the borderlands between the Elven People and the Orcs of Crushbone. Carefully, we picked our way south until we reached a road where we picked up our pace. 
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
Unknown to either of us, an inexperienced raiding party had just run what is called “Orc Hill”. It’s a semi-large encampment of Orc Pawns, Centurions, and Oracles planted directly in between the entrance to their stronghold, Crushbone, and the Tree City of Kelethin. This raiding party managed to attract the attention of nearly every orc on the “Hill” before they made a hasty retreat to the safety of the Militia. The Orcs chased them nearly to the edge of the city before heading back themselves. That’s where they ran into us. 
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
Surrounded by Orcs, Mother yelled for help and tried to delay them. As she lunged at one making a grab for me, another bashed her from behind. Stunned, they ganged up on her. Help arrived all right, just a moment too late for her. She died within sight of her birth city. The city we traveled months and crossed countless lands to reach. 
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
I’m told that they had to pull me from an Oracle. That I’d clawed my way up him and had my legs wrapped around his neck, my fingers in an eye and tearing an ear off with my teeth. I don’t remember it. 
<br />

<br />
The one who rescued me was a ranger. Not knowing what to do with me, he brought me to his trainer. I spent the rest of my growing years in her company, learning her ways. Now, I have the skills that my father and mother lacked. Eventually I put the grief of my mother behind me with the help of Ran Sunfire (and nearly a thousand orcs). My father’s is but one step away, I’m not skilled enough yet for that though. But soon. 
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
Now, when I look back on that, I can’t help but be reminded of how lucky I am to be alive. Invariably, I too am reminded of the first time I saw the trees of Greater Fay. If you’ve not seen them, do. You’ll instantly understand why the Elves love them. And from there, I think of the beauty of the places I’ve seen. The rising geysers and strange mechanical things of Steamfont, the lush green mountains and dwarven stone carvings of Butcher Block, the sun glinting off the waves in the Ocean of Tears, the dangerous crags of Dagnor’s Cauldron, the constantly shifting sands and remote oasis of the Desert of Ro, the grassy plains of the Commonlands. 
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
I’ve seen much beauty thanks to The Mother and with her grace, I’ll see much more.
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
Well, I thank you for the warmth of your fire and the drink of your wine, but my eyes grow heavy as The Mother has cast the sands of sleep into them. If I’m not here in the morning, do not be alarmed. I rise early and tend to wander with the Sun. If we see each other again, it’ll be good fortune. &lt;With that; Morani gently rises smiling, turns her back to the fire and glides to her bed roll. A gentle breeze blows  and seems to bring her voice back in a softly lilting refection; “And then again, maybe I’ll stay…”
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
 
<br />
Morani
<br />
Wanderer of Tunare,
<br />
Protector of The Mother's childern.<i>Edited by: Morani at: 2/8/02 1:59:05 pm
<br />
</i><br />_________________<br />Morani 
<br />
Wanderer of Tunare, 
<br />
Protector of The Mother's children.</span><span class="gentblsmall"></span></td>
