		<td width="150" align="left" valign="top" class="row2"><span class="name"><a name="9123"></a><b>Morani</b></span><br /><span class="postdetails">Vizier<br /><img src="images/Ranks/swords/mod6sword.gif" alt="Vizier" title="Vizier" border="0" /><br /><img src="images/avatars/9388702053ed68d7e75318.jpg" alt="" border="0" /><br /><br />Joined: 19 Aug 2002<br />Posts: 457<br />Location: Michigan, USA</span><br /></td>
		<td class="row2" width="100%" height="28" valign="top"><table width="100%" border="0" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0">
			<tr>
				<td width="100%"><a href="viewtopic.php?p=9123&amp;sid=c2fe5f1ae2cca10913afe104dcbfa14f#9123"><img src="templates/Chronicles/images/icon_minipost.gif" width="12" height="9" alt="Post" title="Post" border="0" /></a><span class="postdetails">Posted: Thu Jun 13, 2002 5:29 pm<span class="gen">&nbsp;</span>&nbsp; &nbsp;Post subject: </span></td>
				<td valign="top" align="right" nowrap="nowrap"><a href="posting.php?mode=quote&amp;p=9123&amp;sid=c2fe5f1ae2cca10913afe104dcbfa14f"><img src="templates/Chronicles/images/lang_english/icon_quote.gif" alt="Reply with quote" title="Reply with quote" border="0" /></a>   </td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td colspan="2"><hr /></td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody">        People who love me die or disappear on me. So many people I thought should love me have looked down on me. OK. Yes. I know that’s not really me speaking anymore. Fine! Still, you have to agree that I have had reason to be cautious. And maybe you’re right. Maybe that’s not what my future is to be anymore. Still, I have to be cautious and I have always been cautious about it. Yet I still don’t understand how someone can tell me they love me. I’ve been careful to not let people get to know me that well so I don’t have to deal with it. I KNOW, we been through this before, I DO wear my heart on my sleeve and that’s been the source of a lot of my hurts. But how can someone tell me they love me and MEAN it? It’s not that I don’t believe, eyes tell the truth and even someone who’s <u>good</u> at lying can’t fake that openness that the eyes can show. 
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
        The eyes, how many times have I looked into those eyes and not seen what I was shone this time? All the time that’s passed and not once did I look for it. What scares me more about that? That it was there for that long and I never knew or that I never looked? Either one is scary and I really don’t want to see that about myself. We’re always the most comfortable with what we know. I know the things that have given me misery and pain. And <u>now</u>, I have to look at them and see that maybe there’s more to it than that. Maybe I’ve been shortchanging them. Maybe I need to give them a chance and risk hurting again. 
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
        Never looking before, now that’s a different kind of fear. Did I know and just pretended I didn’t? I suppose I could have known somewhere and not looked because I <u>knew</u> it would scare me to recognize it.  Or maybe I just thought I’d need to make someone love me for it to happen and it felt safe knowing <b>  I</b> was in control. Well, <b>  I</b> was definitely NOT in control here and when I came down to it, I caved because that’s exactly what I wanted; to not be in control. Did I know? Maybe. But I guess that really doesn’t matter now. I just don’t like the idea that I might have been this off-handed with someone else’s heart. Not after how many times and how long mine’s been torn apart. Maybe <b>  that’s</b> what’s really bothering me here; I’ve been careless with someone else’s heart and I KNOW what it’s like to be hurt. Yes, that does <b>  NOT</b> sit well with me.
<br />

<br />
 
<br />
